.gitattributes text eol=lf

# The contract system declares `canonical_byte_surface: git_blob_utf8_lf`, and its
# validators hash or assert working-tree bytes against that surface — WP6.1 rejects
# non-LF artifacts outright ("artifact must use canonical LF bytes"), while WP6.2 and
# WP6.3 compare `sha256(bytes)` against pinned `*_lf_sha256` values. On a checkout with
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) git materialises these LF blobs as CRLF, so
# every such contract fails locally while passing on any LF checkout, including CI.
#
# These patterns cover the whole class deliberately. This file was previously grown one
# path at a time as each work package hit the failure, which left WP6.1's
# stage1-owner-acceptance-record, all of WP6.2 T3/T4, and all of WP6.3 uncovered — 133
# failing contract tests from a single unset attribute. Every tracked file under these
# roots is committed pure-LF and non-binary, so this changes no committed content.
.research-system/** text eol=lf
tests/research_system/contracts/** text eol=lf

docs/plans/agentic-research-system/design/09-wp6-2-t2-cost-grant-authority-addendum-2026-07-22.md text eol=lf
docs/plans/agentic-research-system/handoffs/trials/gate6-wp6-2-t2-authority-addendum-exact-state-handback.md text eol=lf

# A second LF-critical class, on the same reasoning as the block above but a different
# failure mode: executable scripts. A CRLF shebang is `#!/bin/bash\r`, and a shell that
# does not tolerate it answers `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M` — so the hook does not
# run, and an advisory hook that does not run is indistinguishable from one that ran
# and found nothing. That is the exact silent-absence shape the receipt wrapper and the
# `.git/hooks` vs `.githooks` incident already record.
#
# Not hypothetical: agent edits silently rewrote `.claude/hooks/_receipt-wrap.sh` (276
# lines) and `.claude/settings.json` (79) from LF to CRLF on 2026-09-08, and again
# `tests/tools/test_gitattributes_lf_coverage.py` (143) an hour later, on a checkout
# with `core.autocrlf=false` where nothing had asked for it. Both were caught only by
# reading a diffstat. Until now no pattern here matched `.claude/`, `.codex/`, or
# `.githooks/` at all — including the five git hooks that are the commit-time gate.
#
# Every tracked file under these patterns is committed pure-LF today (verified against
# origin/main), so this changes no committed content. Enforced by
# tests/tools/test_hook_script_lf_bytes.py, which asserts the pin AND the committed
# bytes — the pin alone would not catch a blob that was already CRLF before it landed.
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/** text eol=lf
.claude/hooks/** text eol=lf
.codex/hooks/** text eol=lf

# The hook wiring itself. Not executable, so no shebang to break, but it is the file
# that declares which hooks participate in the enforcement surface, and it was one of
# the two silently rewritten above — a 158-line whole-file diff for a 4-line change is
# a review that does not happen.
.claude/settings.json text eol=lf
